# Official National Park Service web pages: the park's "Gettysburg Overview", an NPS article on the Neutra visitor center and Cyclorama (the transcript of a talk), and a Teaching with Historic Places lesson that reprints a participant's account; fetched 2026-11-16.
# A page of an official body supports only what it literally says. The lesson reprints the account of Andrew Baker, 22nd North Carolina, written for a Confederate veterans' magazine; the extract says whose account it is and carries it as a recollection (hedged), not as a statement of the Service.
# Workstream bt-web, task nps-landscape, third milestone.

@ NPSGOV-HC-GETTYSBURG-OVERVIEW
1 | hospitals | After the battle the wounded and dying were crowded into nearly every building; churches, public buildings and even private homes were hospitals | - | 1863-07 (the aftermath of the battle as the park's overview states it) | stated | The overview says that wounded and dying were crowded into nearly every building and that churches, public buildings and even private homes were hospitals filled with wounded soldiers | Wounded and dying were crowded into nearly every building. >>> filled with wounded soldiers.
1 | burials | Every farm field or garden was a graveyard; most of the dead lay in hastily dug and inadequate graves and some had not been buried at all | - | 1863-07 (the aftermath of the battle as the park's overview states it) | stated | The overview says that every farm field or garden was a graveyard, that most of the dead lay in hastily dug and inadequate graves and that some had not been buried at all | Every farm field or garden was a graveyard. Most of the dead lay in hastily dug and inadequate graves; some had not been buried at all.
1 | hospitals | Camp Letterman General Hospital, east of Gettysburg, took all the wounded before they went to permanent hospitals; under large tents | - | 1863-08 (the general hospital under tents; the overview dates it by its photograph of August 1863 and the departure of the last patients in January 1864) | stated | The overview says Camp Letterman General Hospital was established east of Gettysburg, where all of the wounded were eventually taken before transport to permanent hospitals, that the wards were housed under large tents and that by January 1864 the last patients, surgeons, guards, nurses, tents and cookhouses were gone, only a temporary cemetery on the hillside remaining | To meet the demand, Camp Letterman General Hospital was established east of Gettysburg >>> Only a temporary cemetery on the hillside remained as a testament | Tents of the General Hospital at Camp Letterman, August 1863
1 | cemetery | The Soldiers' National Cemetery, designed by the landscape architect William Saunders, dedicated on November 19, 1863; the removal of the Union dead began in the fall of 1863 | - | !autumn 1863 (the cemetery ground was laid out after the battle) | stated | The overview says David Wills coordinated the establishment of the Soldiers' National Cemetery, designed by the landscape architect William Saunders, that the removal of the Union dead to it began in the fall of 1863 and that the cemetery grounds were dedicated on November 19, 1863 | Gettysburg lawyer David Wills was appointed the state agent to coordinate the establishment of the new >>> dedicated on November 19, 1863.

